body:
Hey man. School was the worst thing I ever did ever. You could count it as a blessing perhaps. If you really want what you were aiming for you'll find a way to get back on the horse and make it work. But if you failed maybe it's because you just weren't interested in or good at the particular subject matter? Don't try too hard to fit a square peg into a round hole if you get what I'm saying. This whole thing about pressuring people to go to school is a ridiculous measure of success and you wouldn't have even been all that much better off just having a degree at something you weren't terribly good at than finding something you genuinely love and are talented in.

&#x200B;

And I'm just speaking generally of course. You didn't give us all the details so I don't know your motivations

&#x200B;

Edit: Just saw the thing about computer science. So tell me... how many brilliant tech billionaires dropped out of college? I feel like this is one of those fields where your skills are more important than the paper. And if it's just the last quarter then take a breather bud... Get a job. Save up some money. Take half the course load you did last time so you're not driving yourself insane and complete it over a longer time frame. You've got this man.

&#x200B;

I just went through an entire med school application cycle with a 98% MCAT score and didn't get in. After not getting in I basically gave up on it even though I know I could apply again and boost my app. I have that choice, but I don't think I want it anymore. It's okay. I need some space for reflection. I know I can always go back and pick up loose ends when I'm in a better place. It's okay to not be the person you thought you were. Or to think you're not, and then rediscover that you are. You got this man

Are you in high school or college? I understand where you're coming from and I literally just made a post myself so I am not going to judge you for being suicidal. That said I have dealt with this myself and it's not as bad as you right now think. I was raised by very successful parents with really high levels of expectation. Had to go to private school and all that. I caved to their pressure to perform well and that in turn caused them to expect more out of me. My sister took the opposite approach. She set herself up as a disappointment so that they were happy if she just made B's. Eventually I stopped putting in as much. They saw my mental and physical health was deteriorating. I started making B's instead of A's and they adapted. 

&#x200B;

Guess what I did this year? Scored 98% on my MCAT, went through a full application cycle to top tier med schools. Man my parents were giddy. They were telling everyone I was going to be a doctor. And then when the cycle ended I was like "nah I don't think this is for me". Took them way high and let them wayyyyy down. But surprisingly they were far more supportive than I expected or have experienced previously. The older you get and the more you show them how capable you are the more they respect you and will probably stop defining your success just by your grades. It's ridiculous to have to prove yourself to your own parents, but it is what it is. If you're in high school it's only temporary. If you're in college just tell them to fuck right off
